"Dating the Surface of Mars | VirtualDemos",
"Dating the Surface of Mars",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> Count craters: Southern highlands, Northern plains, Tharsis lava plains, Olympus Mons caldera (Mars), five lunar surfaces and a Mystery surface. Make a crater: Meteor Crater, Tycho, Copernicus, Chicxulub, Mead, Orientale, Caloris, Hellas, a {#} m asteroid. Cooling: Just formed, When the lunar maria erupted, Today.",
"The demo has three parts, chosen at the top of the picture: Make a crater, Count craters and Cooling. Each has a 3D view and a flat view.",
"<strong>Count craters.</strong> Each surface is a simulated patch of Mars or the Moon, built crater by crater over its age. Click each crater at least as wide as the purple circle, with its center inside the square, to mark it. The graph is the crater clock for Mars: the lunar clock, calibrated with Apollo rocks, carried over to Mars by how often asteroids hit Mars compared with the Moon (Hartmann and Neukum {#}; Ivanov {#}).",
"<strong>Make a crater.</strong> Choose the target, what the asteroid is made of, its diameter, speed and angle, then drop it. The crater’s size comes from a scaling law fitted to experiments and computer models of impacts (Collins, Melosh and Marcus {#}).",
"<strong>Cooling.</strong> A Moon, a Mars and an Earth start equally hot, made of the same rock with the same radioactive elements. The slider moves through the {#} billion years since they formed. The cold lid is the stiff outer layer that heat has to conduct through.",
"Record reading stores what the readouts show (in Cooling, one row for each planet). Export to worksheet sends the readings to the matching table below; Clear starts a new series.",
"Use crater counts to put ages on four regions of Mars, find when Mars was most active, and see why Mars stayed active longer than the Moon but not as long as Earth.",
"<strong>The crater clock.</strong> A surface collects craters as it ages, so the number of craters per square kilometer tells its age. On the Moon the clock is calibrated with rocks of known age brought back by Apollo and Luna. Nobody has yet brought back a rock from a known place on Mars, so the Martian clock is the lunar one adjusted for how often asteroids hit Mars compared with the Moon. Its ages are less certain than the Moon’s, by a few hundred million years for old surfaces.",
"<strong>Mars’s three eras.</strong> Geologists split Mars’s history by its crater counts into the Noachian (heavily cratered, older than about {#} billion years, when water flowed), the Hesperian (about {#} to {#} billion years ago, with widespread lava and floods) and the Amazonian (the last {#} billion years, cold and dry, with fewer craters).",
"<strong>Regions.</strong> The southern highlands are rough and crowded with craters. The northern plains are smooth and low. Tharsis is a huge volcanic bulge carrying the largest volcanoes in the solar system, among them Olympus Mons, whose summit has a caldera (a collapsed crater left by lava draining away) about {#} km across.",
"<strong>Crater size and gravity.</strong> Mars’s gravity is about {#} of Earth’s and about {#} times the Moon’s. Asteroids reach Mars more slowly than they reach the Moon, around {#} km/s against about {#} km/s.",
"Rank the four regions in Background by the age you expect, from oldest to youngest, and give a reason for each.",
